From 407f7e9e3b53413f68d8cdde795cb5eb5ac06796 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: reaper <theunbounddeveloper@outlook.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 08:41:28 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] httpd: a native Site Survey replaces the stock Network Tools
 survey, with a scan that moves a radar-bound radio on request

The stock page ran the prebuilt wlcscan core, whose fixed event buffer keeps
only the first networks in channel order (nothing past channel 44 on a
single 5 GHz radio). Reaper_Survey.asp reads `wl escanresults` text per radio
through a detached worker (httpd/reaper_survey.c parses it in libc; progressive
JSON, 512-network cap, mutually exclusive with capture and Auto Scan). Each
radio reports its own outcome: count and time, passive fallback, own block,
radio off, or the driver's own error text and exit code. Channel occupancy is
drawn as cards per band with this router's channel marked.

A 5 GHz radio under radar monitoring refuses every scan request on this
platform. Plain Scan never moves a radio and says so. "Scan with channel move"
(two-step confirm) does what the stock core does silently: moves a 5 GHz radio
that heard nothing to 36/80 or 149/80 with the chanspec and acs_update iovars
(a bare `wl chanspec` changes only the configured value), scans once the radio
really operates there, and returns through dfs_ap_move when the driver has
background radar checks, so the radio keeps serving during the check. A killed
worker's move is undone at the next start. The worker resets SIGCHLD: it
inherited httpd's reaper, so every exit status had read -1.

Mixed hunks in web.c: the Connections feed labels every live bridge subnet as
LAN (not only br0), carries the destination name and the Names switch, and
publishes the router's own bridge addresses. reaper_inject.c and both menu
trees point the stock survey URL at the new page.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
